'''Situated cognition''' is a family of approaches that emphasize how thinking and learning depend on the activity, environment, body and social context in which they occur.

== Theoretical origins ==
Brown, Collins and Duguid (1989) argued that knowledge is partly constituted by the activity and culture in which it is developed and used, with implications for cognitive apprenticeship. Later reviews describe several overlapping situated approaches: embodied, enacted, social and distributed cognition. They differ in how strongly they reject internal representations or extend cognition beyond the individual.

== Operational hypotheses ==
Contrast performance under authentic and decontextualized tasks while matching content, prior experience and incentives. Vary access to relevant tools or social support; measure both immediate performance and transfer to a new context. A context effect may arise from retrieval cues, affordances, familiarity or collaboration. Distinguish these explanations rather than treat an effect as proof of an entire theoretical framework.

== Applications and boundaries ==
Educational design may include authentic practice, modeling, scaffolding and gradual transfer. Robotics can exploit environmental structure to reduce internal computation, but this does not establish human-like cognition. Situated cognition concerns the significance of context; distributed cognition more specifically studies coordination across people and artifacts, and embodied cognition emphasizes bodily processes. The distinctions are analytical, with overlap among traditions.

